#{[{:activity/_scope [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230002} :activity/id #uuid "bb024668-aa54-5547-86a8-56f97596b95b" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230002 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230188 :tutorial/content "Matrix exponentials can be used for solving homogeneous systems of the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant square matrix.\n\nFor example, consider the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=5y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=-5x(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWriting our system in matrix form, we get\n![](images/f9/f94140f8c9fe736ca7c574547658856e85d938eb08064046716be8eb1a2748de.svg)\nwhere ![](images/26/26dcf2c367c915460d09f4f38b40e4f792a06b6ab44291b4739a6935529daf89.svg) So, the matrix of the system is ![](images/90/9000e03ddf617a1e3c4e0eed5da2b846d62e3d68588b601d90643783960d17df.svg)\n\nRecall that given the system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the general solution of the system can be written as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = \\Phi(t) \\, \\mathbf{c},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\Phi(t)$ is a fundamental matrix of the system, and $\\mathbf{c}$ is some constant vector.\n\nSince we have already seen how to compute the standard fundamental matrix, we will not repeat the calculation here. So, computing the standard fundamental matrix $\\Phi_0(t)$ of the system, we get\n![](images/11/11e0da79656b7f8e2c84c6c54e6dd6b54cf53cbb8e746d44dfb13329c3d2ed8f.svg)\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={Φ}_{0}(t)\\,c \\\\  & ={e}^{At}\\,c \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $c_1,c_2 \\in \\mathbb{R}.$\n\n**Note:** Recall that for the general solution of the ordinary differential equation $x'(t)=ax(t),$ we have \n\n$$\nx'(t)=ax(t)\n\\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad\nx(t) = c \\, e^{at}, \n\\quad\nc \\in \\mathbb{R}.\n$$\n\nNow, for the general solution of the matrix differential equation $\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t),$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t)\n\\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = e^{At} \\, \\mathbf{c},\n\\quad\n\\mathbf{c} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n.\n$$\n\nLet's see more examples." :tutorial/id #uuid "978fc2f4-910c-5bef-9ae3-3da9377642ce" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14515 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "c774d65b-c500-58e4-aa9a-e222d3d136ae" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230003}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230003 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229142 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e5bc609b-dc5b-52e9-9f0e-206f32bb829b"} :step/id #uuid "d3b52974-38d7-50fa-b648-b51510a9a617" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230004}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230004 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230193 :tutorial/content "Now, let's find the solution to the initial value problem below\n![](images/31/3111f065d441881e050417b345b34947509f175f00ebd5402963e432d013348e.svg)\n\nWriting our system in matrix form, we get\n![](images/5c/5c4ed0a2e9db0979e4fc0342c2e4db7c23c60cf58293a496fa661ff1c03fccde.svg)\nwhere ![](images/71/7164659247c5290544655b05943d7d998f67cb1cc3b4cc45bab0902e8059bc75.svg) So, the matrix of the system is ![](images/e3/e3f94e8ce2cc2063d469fbac2af87232cc130f788243c1507bfe9972dd4b04c1.svg)\n\nRecall that the general solution of the system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, can be written as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = \\Phi_0(t) \\, \\mathbf{c},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\Phi_0(t) = e^{At}$ is the standard fundamental matrix of the system, and $\\mathbf{c}$ is some constant vector.\n\nEvaluating both sides of $\\mathbf{x}(t) = e^{At} \\mathbf{c}$ at $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & ={Φ}_{0}(0)\\,c \\\\ x(0) & ={e}^{A(0)}\\,c \\\\ x(0) & =I\\,c \\\\ c & =x(0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the solution to our initial value problem is \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = \\Phi_0(t) \\, \\mathbf{x}(0).\n$$\n\nComputing the standard fundamental matrix $\\Phi_0(t)$ of the system, we get\n![](images/fc/fc4e1ba16be420fbe5e53e2dbb4ee6da96c7097bbe4e117315407ec4dc65365f.svg)\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{At}\\,x(0) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{7t} & t{e}^{7t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{7t}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}(3-2t){e}^{7t} \\\\ -2{e}^{7t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "9cb11887-9535-5a54-a180-eb20a7534696" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14529 :tutorial/title "Solving Initial Value Problems"} :step/id #uuid "5206e604-fe74-5b8a-800c-cdb747dc1e28" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230005}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230005 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229143 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3d611c75-818f-508f-a7a4-49edb32baa41"} :step/id #uuid "c93fbf09-3c42-5a66-8e76-1cf0d810f44a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230006}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230006 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230194 :tutorial/content "Next, let's find the solution to the initial value problem \n![](images/93/93f9c58b8a9cea64ba97f43f2831336ea8d38a6fd6165211a54d2822415c1497.svg)\ngiven that ![](images/ee/ee25228bf71d1b7d9d0688af06a7f1335c252310127b403ed0ed37f7fe277c07.svg) where ![](images/3a/3a3ac9326741c0e2390a8e2f079808f0b432b740149ef4b034ffa7568dc33848.svg)\n\nWriting our system in matrix form, we get\n![](images/2b/2b81cf358ffb3df49f327b63138640ac452a249f989353b521be34d531aeffd9.svg)\nwhere ![](images/11/1110f0bdbe784b21683bfb9610ca9c04e5a45755954d49a15124e530feef2002.svg) So, the matrix of the system is ![](images/47/47d8a0214e5ccdf6625a330d23003dec11cbc3a4f35cb2992df29a688963e4b2.svg)\n\nGiven the system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the general solution is $\\mathbf{x}(t) = e^{At} \\, \\mathbf{c}.$ We can determine the constant vector $\\mathbf{c}$ using the initial condition at time $t=t_0{:}$\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t_0) = e^{At_0} \\, \\mathbf{c} \\implies \\mathbf{c} = e^{-At_0} \\, \\mathbf{x}(t_0),\n$$\n\nsince the inverse of $e^{At_0}$ is $e^{-At_0}.$ Plugging this back into our general solution gives the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{At}\\,{e}^{-A{t}_{0}}\\,x({t}_{0}) \\\\  & ={e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our specific problem (with $t_0=1$) is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{A(t-1)}\\,x(1) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7(t-1)) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7(t-1)) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(7(t-1)) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(7(t-1))\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7(t-1)) \\\\ -4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7(t-1))\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "0fe9a975-9d92-5f79-8a67-34af55196fb3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14530 :tutorial/title "Solving Initial Value Problems With Shifted Conditions"} :step/id #uuid "19b34f7c-755c-5e1b-a16f-22bd17d1ecc7" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230007}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230007 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229144 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "37e58bb6-6d90-5171-bba2-6ffa5a0c91d9"} :step/id #uuid "633a401e-5116-5c9e-a0b4-8848cf596d7b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230008}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230008 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230192 :tutorial/content "Before proceeding with the next example, let's recall some important properties of matrix exponentials:\n\n1\\. *Law of Exponents:* If $A$ is a constant square matrix, then\n\n  $$\n  e^{A(t+s)} = e^{At}\\,e^{As}.\n  $$\n\n2\\. *Inverse:* For any $t,$ the matrix $e^{At}$ is invertible and \n\n  $$\n  (e^{At})^{-1} = e^{-At}.\n  $$\n\n3\\. *Identity:* For any square matrix $A,$\n\n  $$\n  e^{A\\cdot 0} = I.\n  $$\n\n4\\. *Product of exponentials:* If $A$ and $B$ commute (i.e., $AB = BA$), then\n\n  $$\n  e^{A}e^{B} = e^{A+B}.\n  $$\n\n  **Watch out!** In general, this identity is *not true* unless $A$ and $B$ commute.\n\nLet's see how these properties can be used in practice." :tutorial/id #uuid "5ae3cfa7-a5a5-5a50-aefa-8b8dec561567" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14528 :tutorial/title "Reminder: Properties of Matrix Exponentials"} :step/id #uuid "eb56047e-3845-5f8e-9282-d41f308270e3" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230009}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230009 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229145 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1ff837c0-0ebb-5a9e-acb4-894261e0318c"} :step/id #uuid "6a90e198-ec80-5daf-8cb9-69841a16a32d"}] :activity/title "Solving Homogeneous Systems of ODEs Using Matrix Methods" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186229256} {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482627} :activity/id #uuid "14357ccb-7387-5d90-95d5-9ec0c77cd805" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482627 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482598 :question/id #uuid "d66735c3-ba98-5f1c-8e1d-7d80133c59cf"} :step/id #uuid "2379b8ac-5990-5a88-876d-7a3b13ce4148" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482645}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482629 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482509 :question/id #uuid "1163c64f-8eb6-5387-bd44-2f1d21a98032"} :step/id #uuid "28f5bdee-7eed-595c-9820-abd9f0486f9a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482640}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482640 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482614 :question/id #uuid "f598b188-bddf-5e21-881f-0c355a5c9889"} :step/id #uuid "7f8b56f9-e657-5fae-8f23-51af234be7a5"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482645 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482582 :question/id #uuid "b5cac624-fcaa-5546-985b-94ad2df95205"} :step/id #uuid "b0d055da-4dd2-51f2-ae55-dae45284bf27" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482648}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482648 :step/content {:db/id #edb