orresponding coefficient matrix are linearly independent.\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2} &=6 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} &=12 \\\\ -2{x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-2{x}_{3} &=12\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}}}=\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}}}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\hat{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\hat{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 9\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}54 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}54 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394717 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "022fd928-0862-5733-ab58-6ac021653bf2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\